{
  "claims": [
    {
      "component_id": "C1",
      "text": "The fMRI acceleration method addressed the time-consuming obstacle that traditional MRI methodologies require full k-space information, acquired through a unique data-acquisition sequence, to reconstruct the complete image; the proposed approach combines through-plane and in-plane acceleration techniques to accelerate image acquisition along two dimensions and uses multiple image-shift strategies together with a 2D Hadamard encoding scheme to increase encoding diversity and reduce slice leakage.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41951145"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E4", "E5", "E7", "E8"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C2",
      "text": "Applying the Bayesian 2D acceleration approach to both simulated and experimental fMRI data reduced total scan time while achieving a higher signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) and contrast-to-noise ratio (CNR) in regions of interest (ROI), and compared with conventional reconstruction strategies the proposed method demonstrated potential improvements in activation detection under specific acceleration and encoding conditions while also providing voxel-wise estimates through a Bayesian framework.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41951145"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E9", "E10"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C3",
      "text": "The MR anisotropic-diffusion work addressed Rician denoising, which is important but complex because of the inherent correlation between Rice's noise and the MR signal during MR image formation and can prove insufficient and even result in loss of information details; to overcome these two drawbacks, PDE-based filtering methods were introduced but their filter parameters must be determined with great accuracy. The authors proposed adapted anisotropic-diffusion PDE filter models with precise local parameter adjustment for denoising MR images with high Rice's noise levels, incorporating a robust Rician noise estimator and an unbiased variant of anisotropic-diffusion-based filters whose parameters are all calculated simultaneously and optimized adaptively through the PSO algorithm using reference-based and no-reference quality metrics (PSNR, SSIM and Blind/Referenceless Image Spatial Quality Evaluator (BRISQUE)), yielding three unbiased anisotropic diffusion filtering models with optimized parameters for Rician noise removal in MR images.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41903663"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E12", "E13", "E15", "E16", "E11"]
    },
    {
      "component_id": "C4",
      "text": "The various tests performed on synthetic and real MR images provided excellent results compared to some other published methods, demonstrating the high performance of the proposed anisotropic-diffusion filters in terms of noise reduction and preservation of important details.",
      "cite": ["PMID:41903663"],
      "evidence_ids": ["E17"]
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "evidence_status": "complete",
  "evidence_gap": ""
}

